cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "The Erdős–Lovász cover number problem: g(6) = 17, and new lower bounds for every r"
abstract: >-
  The case r = 6 of the Erdos-Lovasz cover number problem, posed in 1974 in the
  paper that introduced the local lemma: the minimum number of edges g(r) in an
  r-uniform intersecting hypergraph with cover number r. The values known exactly
  are g(3) = 6, g(4) = 9 and g(5) = 13; the strongest published general lower
  bound gives g(6) >= 14.
  A computer-assisted determination of g(6), the smallest number of blocks in a
  pairwise intersecting 6-uniform family with transversal number 6. Families of at
  most fifteen cards are excluded by two independent routes; sixteen cards are
  excluded by a finite reduction to 463 canonical cores whose SAT instances are all
  unsatisfiable with independently checked DRAT certificates; and an explicit
  seventeen-card family on 27 symbols gives the matching upper bound. Hence
  g(6) = 17. Research draft: no priority is claimed, and the lower bound is
  computer-assisted and has not been independently reviewed by a human referee or a
  proof assistant. AI tools were used throughout the work and its review.
  Two further papers give proposed proofs, unreviewed outside this project:
  g(r) >= 3r - 3 for every r and g(r) >= 3r - 2 for every r >= 16, and a new
  asymptotic lower bound liminf g(r)/r >= 3.1108, above the constant 3.0534 of
  Sivashankar (arXiv:2606.24878).
type: software
